# When a Bill Was Introduced to Prevent Objectionable Advertisements
Tamil title: தகாத முறையிலே ஒட்டப்படுகிற விளம்பரங்களைத் தடுக்கச் சட்டம் கொண்டு வந்தபோது

Speech delivered on 17 February 1959.

**Arignar Anna:** Mr Speaker, like Sardar Vedaratnam Pillai, who spoke before me, I regret that those responsible for the government have been reduced to introducing a separate law to prevent advertisements pasted in an objectionable manner and, where such advertisements provoke obscenity, to suppress them. While proclaiming the good aim of preventing obscenity, they are bringing in a law framed so that this aim cannot be properly fulfilled. I regret that too.

The rulers bring this law as though they have accepted responsibility for the supposed increase of obscenity in this country, as though they alone know what obscenity is and have been entrusted with suppressing it in every form, while many others are producing it. Yet obscenity varies according to each person's outlook and changes from age to age. At a time when ideas are taking shape, restrictive laws are not merely contrary to democracy; they are a great obstacle to the growth of reason.

Twenty or twenty-five years ago, the entry of oppressed communities into temples was regarded not merely as obscene but as a great sin. Learned scholars declared that a temple would lose its sanctity if they entered it. Those scholars repeated what they had learned rather than speaking in accordance with the world. A Temple Entry Act therefore had to be enacted to create the just condition in which oppressed people received their proper rights.

Whenever a new idea is first expressed, people call it obscene and predict many disasters. Afterwards, as the English phrase says, there is a “survival of the fittest”: the propaganda possessing life and strength survives, while the other propaganda fails.

An honourable member argued that people are neither deluded nor deceived by what our side writes, citing his party's numerical strength as proof. If that is what you believe, I cannot understand why a new law is needed.

When we pointed out that the existing law already covers the matter, the member for Lalgudi said that section 295-A was insufficient. He then searched for the Indian Penal Code. Unfortunately he could not find it; fortunately I could. I wish to draw attention to the words showing that the present law is adequate:

> 295-A Whoever with deliberate and malicious intension of outraging the religious feelings of any class of citizens of India, by words, either spoken or written, or by visible representations insults or attempts to insult the religion or the religious beliefs of that class, shall be punished with imprisonment.

That is what the section says. To understand the purpose of this legislation, I should relate briefly the history behind it. The source prints that an outline was presented by a private member in 1965, and then says that the private member's 1956 bill sought to prevent walls and towns being disfigured by indiscriminate poster-pasting. The Government seized the opportunity, promised to introduce it as a government bill, and inserted many provisions the member had never contemplated, though he may support them today. Those provisions leave me unwilling even to examine its clauses before strongly condemning its purpose.

If the world asks who you are to prevent obscenity, you may declare what is or is not obscene only if the world accepts that you are qualified to know the difference.

Several members cited cinema posters. A Tamil woman shown in a poster for a Tamil film may be considered obscene here to a degree that would not apply elsewhere. When a North Indian woman is shown, people in Tamil Nadu may dismiss it as customary there. Tamil grammar already has the convention called `இடக்கரடக்கல்`, speaking of an improper thing indirectly. Bound by that convention, I shall leave you to infer what I mean.

Ideas vary from country to country and age to age. As members on our side said, this bill resembles pounding the mortar while thinking of flattened rice: I openly accuse you of acting from a desire to discover every possible means of obstructing opposition propaganda.

I ask whether their existing strength has ever been used to remove obscenity. How many tar-written walls have Congress volunteers whitewashed after explaining to the public what was obscene? How many householders or public-spirited volunteers have erased such writing because it disgraced the town or incited violence?

**Sri M. R. Kandaswami Mudaliyar:** I rise on a point of order, Sir. The honourable member asks where Congress workers removed obscene writing. Complaints were received from many places in Salem district and such writing on walls was removed. It cannot be denied. Therefore his statement is wrong.

**Mr Speaker:** This is not a point of order. The honourable member is giving information.

**Sri M. R. Kandaswami Mudaliyar:** I intervened because the honourable member is making an incorrect statement.

**Mr Speaker:** The honourable member cannot say that.

**Anna:** Mr Speaker, if the Government would establish a volunteer corps for this purpose, I am prepared on behalf of our movement to enlist as many of our members in it as possible. No one lacks concern that obscenity should be removed. But this law enables cases to be fabricated according to local likes, dislikes, and political animosities. Whether you intend it or not, it will indirectly be used to suppress opposition propaganda.

I hope the Government will not be angry if I draw attention to the English expression “writing on the wall.” By seeing what is written on walls, rulers can learn what the people think. From Julius Caesar's time to the age of Lord Macmillan, Western countries have allowed such words. Why panic at something written on a wall and insist that it be prohibited?

Opposite our movement's `Arivagam` in Royapuram stands a large wall. A party hostile to us wrote, “Is one who betrayed others an artist?” I ordered that it should not be whitewashed. If people formed an adverse opinion of us after reading it, we should bow to public opinion. I did not say its presence disgraced the movement. Why do these rulers lack that courage? Why do they not believe objectionable writing can be defeated through propaganda? Do they lack propaganda artillery or rapid-fire weapons? Why not use their many forms of publicity to remove it?

I was deeply saddened when the respected Swami Sahajananda spoke with tears about where this might lead. I tell him and others like him that the world has already judged many deeply held beliefs unfit for future generations. However profound your faith, those beliefs are being eroded by changes in the world. Do not say that criticism wounds your feelings. If conviction is firm, the mind will not be wounded. You must prevent change, adapt to it, or withdraw from it; endlessly commanding “Do not do this or that” is no answer.

The Government has the numbers to pass this bill. Precisely when it possesses such strength, restraint should be greatest. Its glory would lie in saying, “We could enact this law, but do not wish to exercise our power that way.” Instead, one member spoke as though Congress had distilled and bestowed rights and democracy upon unworthy people for ten years, and they had failed to use the gift properly. I reflected on which side that member stood when those rights were created.

To say, “We gave you rights for ten years and you failed to use them properly,” reveals an authoritarian instinct. The Lalgudi member even suggested that this bill was insufficient and that there should be a law forbidding such speech altogether. If you can survive only by taking our thumb and cutting out our tongue, do it openly. You too have thumbs and tongues.

You have Sardar Vedaratnam, learned in morality. Why not prevent obscenity through his righteous path, through moral instruction, and through the guidance of Valluvar's Kural, rather than by law? Giving fresh powers to the police places democracy in the furnace.

Intellectuals and politicians are entrusting this authority to the police. Whether writing on a wall is criminal depends on its nature. Minister Bhaktavatsalam, responsible for law and order, explained that writing is not an offence if it neither conflicts with religion nor wounds feelings. But consider to whom this power is delegated. Many police constables cannot reliably distinguish objectionable writing from lawful writing; they will file cases and leave advocates later to debate how it might incite violence.

Swami Sahajananda referred to the slogan “Brahmins, go out.” I neither relish nor welcome it. Yet it admits two interpretations. One might say it alleges wrongdoing and foreignness and simply asks Brahmins to leave. A government advocate might say the imperative “go out” incites action and implies, “If you do not leave, we shall drive you out.” Courts can argue endlessly over such implications. Why is a new law necessary?

Members said many petitions had been received. Does the ruling party attend equally to every petition? A petition saying there is no tank goes into the waste-paper basket. One saying there is no manure is not even glanced at. A petition about inadequate irrigation receives no concern. Yet the moment a petition arrives on this subject, the Government displays remarkable zeal. I congratulate its skill.

How many prosecutions have been brought under the existing IPC provision? Has anyone been imprisoned? Has any effort been made to use it? Nothing has happened to explain this failure. Much is written today, and some words will displease some people. But “we dislike it, therefore it is wrong and offensive” is a false argument. Many unwelcome words must necessarily be spoken.

An old generation is rapidly disappearing and a new generation is forming in the womb. The transitional generation is afraid to retain faith in old things. Amid competing ideas and forms of propaganda, sound ideas will survive; obsolete ones will not.

Several members asked whether morality was unnecessary. Those who proclaim morality do not alone desire it, nor do those who reject their prescribed method reject morality. The meaning assigned to morality changes with time. I recently read that the Finance Minister once considered the double plaits worn by young girls obscene but now finds them attractive. Cropped hair was once called obscene; English itself was once considered a base language, although many among us now adopt English manners and dress.

The member for Vellore, an expert in Tamil literature, said everything contrary to old civilisation was immoral. The world now understands that this is not so. Immorality lies in conduct that makes others live in hardship and loss and destroys their lives. How can everything I like be moral and everything I dislike immoral?

Our respected friend A. Govindasamy cited old stories whose ideas do not accord with reason, including the Tiruvannamalai beacon and the legend of Kannappa Nayanar. Sardar Vedaratnam supplied an explanation without hesitation, and I was genuinely pleased. He evidently realised that merely repeating the Puranic story of Vishnu and Brahma searching for Siva's head and feet would invite laughter and disbelief today, so he sought a scientific explanation. I commend his ability. Old ideas may have stood in their own age, but we must attend to the generation in which we now live and allow new ideas to be expressed freely.

Where expression actually incites violence, cases can be brought under the IPC. This bill instead binds hands in advance. Do not imagine that people will stop writing on walls when it passes. They may write even more. Experience with the law against burning the flag proves it: once the law was enacted, more flags were burned.

**Hon. Mr Bhaktavatsalam:** Everyone knows what happened after the law concerning flag-burning was enacted.

**Anna:** Our honourable Police Minister speaks in the language of the police. A law has value only if, after it is enacted, it creates an impulse not to violate it. Otherwise the Assembly's time is wasted. If every violation merely demands another punishment, there is no end.

You bear great responsibility and possess numerical strength. You should not rush through unnecessary laws that can indirectly injure opposition parties. Despite a severe law and threats of heavy punishment, five thousand people were reportedly prepared to burn the flag. That is no source of governmental pride.

This bill is unnecessary and will not achieve its purpose. If effective methods of persuasion are adopted, our party is ready to offer full cooperation. I respectfully ask the Police Minister not to introduce such legislation.

Other countries do not have this law. I do not claim the authority of one who has lived abroad for seven or eight years; I have never travelled overseas. But I read newspapers and pamphlets about demonstrations. Agitations abroad are no less vigorous than ours. When the American Vice-President visited Mexico, protests demanded that he not come. When a prominent Russian leader visited America, opponents expressed their dissatisfaction through many forms of agitation.

In a democracy where one party governs for five years, people and opposition parties must have ways to express dissatisfaction between elections. Those ways must be inexpensive, simple, and equally available to all parties. That is why wall-writing is used here and abroad, not because of moral depravity.

Some called those who write on walls children. I recalled reading that Lenin carried charcoal in his pocket and wrote about the revolution wherever he found a wall. When great men such as Lenin used this political method, have you become too great to dismiss wall-writing as childish? If wall-writing is to remain free of obscenity, provide better channels of propaganda; this harsh bill is unnecessary.

At other times you object that we speak in alliterative Tamil. Say plainly, “Do not speak. If we are to remain powerful, do not enter politics; if we are to survive, do not speak; if we are to govern, do not write.” Then matters will end smoothly and neither side will be troubled. A law introduced in democracy's name but designed this way destroys genuine democratic feeling.

Even where the law condemns dragging and burning an effigy, people still do so when rain fails. We must consider the intention behind the act rather than forbid the act in every circumstance. The Police Minister, skilled in legal subtleties, may explain intent and decide who is guilty. But does everyone possess his resources, eloquence, and command of words?

If an ordinary person writes his principle on a wall and is prosecuted, how can he explain the precise legal meaning of his words? Has everyone studied the `Tolkappiyam`, `Purananuru`, and `Akananuru?` Does everyone understand legal subtlety? They must hire lawyers. To impose such expense in the hope that they will abandon political activity is an indirect attempt to suppress opposition propaganda.

I do not accuse you of suppressing it entirely. So long as you accept democracy, you cannot. It is not generosity that keeps you from suppressing us; democracy itself prevents you. An Ayub Khan or another dictator could stop propaganda, but then propaganda would cease and bombs would begin.

In democracy, opposition parties possess rights in order to defend themselves. Your pose of lavishly granting those rights from some book of sacred law or charitable endowment is amusing. I respectfully ask the Police Minister immediately to abandon this bill, which indirectly harms democracy.

If his purpose truly is to prevent obscenity, let an all-party body attempt the task for two or five years. If it fails, a later proposal for legislation might at least have the appearance of justification. At present it has not even that. I firmly believe ordinary persuasion can solve the problem.

I read another observation by the Police Minister. Some consider boys and girls studying together obscene; others regard it as a mark of civilisation. Some believe culture requires men and women to sit separately; others outside this House believe no obscenity arises from sitting together. What is obscene or repulsive depends on each person's moral yardstick.

I have read that Cato of Rome, when given authority, included in his moral scheme a ban on gold coins. He feared their glitter would awaken greed and wanted iron coins whose ugliness would repel people from money. Every attempt to preserve morality by command has failed. You overestimate yourselves if you imagine that, like divinely sent apostles, you can preserve this country's morality spotless for another ten years.

In democracy, such overestimation leads toward authoritarianism. Do not take that road. Measure yourselves accurately; legislate according to your capacity; prepare bills according to necessity.

Just as Sardar Vedaratnam believes these matters can be dealt with through moral persuasion without law, you too should acquire that confidence. On behalf of my movement, I respectfully submit that this law is unnecessary. I do not say this because I fear its severe provisions will obstruct our propaganda. We have never written such obscenities, as the Police Minister and others know. Many members also know whom the bill has in mind.

Do not suppose I claim that obscenity does not exist. But when many other means are available, do not manufacture new offenders through legislation and place democracy in the furnace. On other questions, the Government has convened different parties before deciding. Did we not discuss the educational scheme and Panchayat law together? Why not convene leaders and members of every party to devise methods of removing obscenity?

When has the Government attempted such consultation? Have we ever obstructed it? I assure you that we shall support any such effort in future. With that assurance, I respectfully ask the Police Minister not to press this bill.

---NOTES---
The title is descriptive because the source heading records the date and occasion rather than naming the bill formally.

The printed IPC passage uses `intension`; it is retained verbatim in the English translation because the corrected Tamil source preserves the scan. The standard statutory spelling is `intention`.

The source prints `1965` in the bill-history paragraph and immediately refers to the private member's bill introduced in `1956`. The apparent chronological inconsistency is retained and not silently resolved.

`இடக்கரடக்கல்` is a Tamil grammatical and rhetorical convention of referring indirectly to something considered improper to state openly.

`கொடும்பாவி` denotes an effigy ritually dragged or burned, including in folk practices intended to bring rain. `அயூப்கான்` refers to Pakistan's military ruler Ayub Khan.
